<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Frederick IV, Duke of Austria</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1382">1382</a>–<a href="/years/1439">1439</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Frederick of the Empty Pockets · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Frederick IV, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg dynasty</a>, ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and the Habsburg lands in Further Austria. In 1415 he helped the antipope John XXIII flee the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a>, and King Sigismund outlawed him. The Swiss Confederates, led by Bern, then seized the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aargau">Aargau</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-castle">Habsburg Castle</a>, the dynasty's ancestral seat.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/frederick-iv-duke-of-austria.jpg" alt="Frederick IV, Duke of Austria" width="320" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Antoni Boys · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Friedrich_IV_of_Austria.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Frederick was the youngest son of Duke Leopold III and Viridis, a daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernabo-visconti">Bernabò Visconti</a>, Lord of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>. His father died at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-sempach">Battle of Sempach</a> in 1386, and Frederick and his elder brothers William, Leopold IV and Ernest were placed under the tutelage of their uncle Duke Albert III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>. After Albert's death in 1395 the brothers claimed their rights, and when Frederick came of age in 1402 he was assigned the scattered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands in Swabia known as Further Austria and lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/freiburg-im-breisgau">Freiburg im Breisgau</a>. Leopold IV ceded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> to him in 1406, and after Leopold's death in 1411 Frederick became sole ruler in Further Austria.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His early years were marked by conflict. Tyrolean nobles opposed him in 1406 and 1407 and gave him the nickname &quot;of the Empty Pockets&quot;, and he faced a rebellion in the Bishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/appenzell-wars">Appenzell Wars</a>, which ended in peace in 1410 though Appenzell became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> of the Old Swiss Confederacy in 1411. In 1415, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/western-schism">Western Schism</a>, he helped Antipope John XXIII flee the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-constance">Council of Constance</a>. King Sigismund placed him under the Imperial ban, and Frederick lost the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aargau">Aargau</a>, the Freiamt and the County of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden">Baden</a> to the Swiss, though with local support he kept <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a>.</p><p>In 1420 he moved his Tyrolean court from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/merano">Meran</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innsbruck">Innsbruck</a>. His rule there stabilised after several noble rebellions, partly because <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> mining began successfully and brought prosperity. After the death of his brother Ernest on 10 June 1424 he also became regent of Inner Austria for his nephews Frederick V, the later Emperor Frederick III, and Albert VI. In his last years Prince-Bishop Alexander of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trento">Trent</a> instigated another rebellion against his Tyrolean rule.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Frederick died at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innsbruck">Innsbruck</a> on 24 June 1439, a rich man despite his nickname, and was buried in the Cistercian abbey of Stams. He had married Elizabeth of the Palatinate in 1407; she and their infant daughter died shortly after the birth on 27 December 1408. In 1411 he married Anna, daughter of Duke Frederick I of Brunswick-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wolfenbuttel">Wolfenbüttel</a>. Of their children only Sigismund, born in 1427, survived to adulthood. He succeeded his father in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyrol">Tyrol</a> and Further Austria and was called der Münzreiche, &quot;Rich in Coin&quot;.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Frederick IV of Austria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pledged to the Habsburgs in 1330, it regained imperial status in 1415, when Duke <b>Frederick IV</b> of Austria was outlawed during the Council of Constance, and afterwards bought out the remaining claims.</blockquote><a href="/state/canton-of-schaffhausen">Canton of Schaffhausen</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1415, when <b>Duke Frederick IV</b> was outlawed, Bern conquered the Aargau, including Habsburg Castle itself.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-habsburg">County of Habsburg</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/innsbruck">Innsbruck</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.27,11.39&amp;year=1419" aria-label="Innsbruck on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4817 505 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the place of death of Frederick IV, Duke of Austria"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5267 775h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5267 775h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the place of death. <a href="/atlas?at=47.27,11.39&amp;year=1419">What was here in 1419? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
